Project Context:
An app prototype designed for a university entrepreneurship course, TeenCoach is an idea developed by my collaborator and refined amongst a team to connect parents of young children with athletic teens to help
make private sports coaching more accessible, affordable, and personable for kids.

Roles/Responsibilities (Designer & Product Manager):
1. Developed a Figma wireframe and prototype which walks users through the features/process of using Polaris
2. Developed typography and colour palette for app
3. Applied user research to determine the core problem and solution
4. Ideated features based on current alternatives generated by primary competitors and user needs
5. Helped develop the lean canvas model for the app (product)

Core Problem:
Parents with young kids struggle with the rising costs of private sports coaching, leaving them with very few alternatives that are accessible, personable, and provide quality coaching while maintaining affordability.
High youth unemployment leaves teens struggling to find sources of income that allow them to balance their personal schedules with part-time jobs, as well as finding parents who are willing to pay for their services.

Research Methods:
1. Determined customer segment - pivot from ice hockey to indoor dryland sports with a focus on basketball
2. Developed a UVP - analyzed current market competitors to determine product improvements
3. Surveys with parents and teens to understand customer pain points, necessary in-app features, and user concerns
4. Interviewed potential users and competitors to improve in-app safety, coach validation, and UX/UI design
5. Advisor pitch meetings to discuss potential product considerations, challenges, and next steps
6. Interviews with users to validate prototype designs

Final Design Solution:
Our mobile app TeenCoach allows parents to match their children with teen coaches for a unique sports mentorship experience. Our app aims to make coaching feel personable and engaging for parents. We embrace the uniqueness of every child, understanding that a supportive coaching experience can often start with the right mentor. This is why parents are given matches based on their desired sport, experience level, and location, allowing them to view a variety of matches.

Unique Value Proposition (UVP):
Our proposed solution is a sports-coaching app which helps parents who are interested in engaging their child in sports by matching them with experienced teens who provide relatability, comfort, and skill proficiency. This product outperforms current market competitors as it creates a unique mentorship experience for younger athletes by providing accessibility, personability, and engagement to our target customer segment.

Competitive Landscape:
1. Recreation centres
     - Pros: affordable, direct feedback to improve
     - Cons: limited one-on-one attention
2. YouTube
     - Pros: affordable, private
     - Cons: lacks direct feedback to improve
3. Private club coaching
     - Pros: private, direct feedback to improve
     - Cons: unaffordable

Why An App?
1. Accessibility - 94% of people among Gen Z use a smartphone as their preferred device
2. Comfortability - A mobile app provides ease and simplicity that creates convenience for our target audience who have busy schedules
3. Familiarity - The user interface (UI) is designed to feel familiar to other common mobile marketplace apps (i.e. Airbnb, Uber), which connect dual users through quick and efficient bookings

Product Differentiation (Features based on User Research):
1. Safety - discussion with recreation centre coach to determine best practices for implementing safety measures: app requires first aid and vulnerable sector checks to be completed before processing
2. Coach Validity - interviews/surveys with parents determined the need for teens to complete ID/age verification as well as submit sport certifications
3. Feedback & Ratings - interviews/surveys with parents and teens revealed the need to have a positive feedback loop to encourage bookings as well as teens wanting progress tracking to find ways they can improve their
                        coaching sessions: implemented a rating system as well as direct feedback that will be accessible to parents and teens to support transparency and in-app reliability
4. Chats - interviews/surveys with parents indicated the need to be able to communicate with teens after booking a session: chat feature was implemented with a "locked chat system"
           -> chats lock 30 min after a coaching session to protect teens using the app against unwanted messages from parents (UX consideration)
           -> advisor feedback suggested that parents may try to reach out to teens to book outside the app, but the locked chats force re-bookings with coaches to continue conversations (business consideration)
5. AI-Generated Lesson Content - surveys with teens indicated a customer pain point of scheduling/time management: lesson content helps guide teens through coaching so they can focus on building an experience for kids

Channels:
1. Social Media - connect with teens through short-form video content/posts
2. In-Person Channels - QR codes on posters within the community (i.e. local rec. spaces), word-of-mouth

Revenue & Cost Structure:
Implementation of a marketplace model with a 10% service fee on coach revenue per transaction. Estimated start-up capital of $15,000 for Year 1, given costs for app development, AI integration, social media management, and 
app maintenance fees. Leveraging sweat equity allows our team to reduce costs for the UX/UI design and app development.

Reflection:
Working on this project provided insight into applying user research to develop a robust user experience that was tangible and practical for our customer segment. As the sole designer for this app, there were several UX
considerations when designing to solve key user pain points. The continuous iteration of the design was the most valuable part of this project, as it allowed me to conceptualize user problems and find unique solutions to
create a dynamic user experience.

My original wireframe had a very different design compared to the final product from changes to typography, layout, visual hierarchy, and features. However, the implementation of these changes was not simply based on my
design ethos and aesthetics, but were influenced by real user data that was collected, analyzed, and refined to create a user interface that exceeded user needs and outperformed our product's competitors.

The iterative nature of the design process forced me to consider not only UX considerations but business considerations that were essential for the product, such as the "locked chat" feature implemented to support parents 
while ensuring that we retained our customer segment. Learning to explore UX from the lens of a designer and a product manager encouraged me to understand how I could improve the solution for users while building a strong
business model for the product.
